<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Delville Wood</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Delville Wood was fought from 15 July to 3 September 1916 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-somme">Battle of the Somme</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The 1st South African Infantry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> was ordered to take the wood and hold it at all costs, and clung on for six days under intense German shelling and counter-attacks; of more than 3,000 men who went in, only about 750 came out unhurt. The South African National Memorial now stands there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-delville-wood.jpg" alt="Battle of Delville Wood" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SVG version, and changes: Grandiose; Original map:Worldwari map13 largerview.jpg, US Military; Inset map: Giro720, File:France blank1.svg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Battle_of_the_Somme,_1916.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Delville Wood, a thick tangle of beech and hornbeam with hazel thickets cut by grassy rides, lay east of the village of Longueval. In the general offensive that began on 14 July 1916, the Battle of Bazentin Ridge, General Douglas Haig meant to take the German second position between Delville Wood and Bazentin le Petit. The divisions of XIII and XV Corps of General Sir Henry Rawlinson's Fourth Army attacked at 3:25 a.m. after a five-minute hurricane bombardment, which gained a measure of surprise. Longueval could not be held safely without the wood, which overlooked the village and offered the Germans a starting point for counter-attacks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The 1st South African Infantry Brigade, fighting its first battle on the Western Front with the 9th (Scottish) Division, took the wood on 15 July. The village and wood formed a salient that German artillery could fire on from three sides. A South African attack on 17 July failed, and in the night the German 3rd Guards Division advanced behind a creeping barrage and drove the brigade from its forward trenches. On 20 July troops of the 76th Brigade broke through, and Lieutenant-Colonel E. F. Thackeray marched out with two wounded officers and 140 other ranks, the last of the brigade.</p><p>For the rest of July and August British attacks and German counter-attacks continued. Wet weather, ammunition shortages and heavy losses reduced both sides to piecemeal attacks on narrow fronts, most of them stopped by defensive fire and mud. The fighting went on for seven weeks, until just before the Battle of Flers–Courcelette.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The 9th (Scottish) Division lost 7,517 men from 1 to 20 July, 2,536 of them from the South African brigade, and the 3rd Division lost 6,102 from 11 to 27 July. Holding Longueval and the wood let the divisions to the north take High Wood, and the Battle of Flers–Courcelette, with the first use of tanks, began from there in September. The wood, replanted with oak and birch by the South African government, keeps the remains of trenches, a museum and the South African National Memorial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Delville Wood” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…led the campaign that forced the German surrender in South West Africa in July 1915, and South African troops fought in German East Africa under Smuts and on the Western Front, where the South African Brigade suffered heavy losses at the <b>Battle of Delville Wood</b> in July 1916.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
